x86_64-linux  Two differences, and neither is a name spelled differently. The clang-only pair is `__cxa_free_exception` and `__cxa_get_exception_ptr`: the first is the cleanup clang lays down for a throw whose *object's own constructor* throws, freeing the storage `__cxa_allocate_exception` handed back before it was ever thrown, and the second is what the Itanium ABI asks a handler to call before `__cxa_begin_catch` when it copy-initialises a by-value parameter. cxx1 emits neither - it copies through the pointer `__cxa_begin_catch` returns, which is the same pointer, and it has no cleanup around the construction of a thrown object. The one this case can be read as being about, `__cxa_end_catch`, agrees: both compilers call it on every way out of a handler, which is what the ledger line measures. The cxx1-only `_ZN1EC1Ei`, `_ZN1EC1ERKS_` and `_ZN1ED1Ev` are the older trap - clang emits only the C2 and D2 forms of a constructor and destructor defined inside their class on this target, where cxx1 emits the complete-object forms beside them, identical bodies for a class with no virtual base. move-constructor.nonames and reference-temporary-lifetime.nonames record the same thing.
arm64-darwin  Only the clang-only pair, `___cxa_free_exception` and `___cxa_get_exception_ptr`, for the reasons the x86_64-linux line gives. This target emits both constructor forms whatever the definition's place, so the C1/D1 half of that line does not arise here, and every other name agrees.
